On May 28, 5:19 pm, Garrison Benson <Benson.Garri...@gmail.com> wrote:
> For the past two years I've run a small NetHack tournament for some
> friends on an old Pentium III machine I have lying around. This year,
> I want to run dgamelaunch so we can watch each other play games.
> (Also, it's nice that it handles the users.)
>
> Anyway, I've been having some trouble getting it set up properly. It
> works fine when I run dgamelaunch directly (as root), but when I run
> it through telnet, problems arise... upon telnetting to the server, I
> get this message:
>
> in.telnetd: invalid option -- q
> Usage: telnetd [-debug port] [-D (options|report|exercise|netdata|
> ptydata)] [-h] [-L login_program] [-n]
>
> My telnet configuration line in /etc/inetd.conf is the following:
> telnet stream tcp nowait root.root /usr/sbin/tcpd /usr/sbin/in.telnetd
> -h -L /usr/sbin/dgamelaunch -q -f /etc/dgamelaunch.conf
>
> My guess is that it thinks the -q argument is supposed to go to
> in.telnetd, rather than dgamelaunch. How can I tell it which is the
> right one? (PS, I'm kind of a Linux noob.) Thanks in advance.

Knowing nothing about the particular setup or running dgamelaunch, try
this:

telnet stream tcp nowait root.root /usr/sbin/tcpd /usr/sbin/in.telnetd
-h -L '/usr/sbin/dgamelaunch -q -f /etc/dgamelaunch.conf'

The -L option probably expects only one argument, instead of consuming
the rest of the line.

